<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How The Body Explained researches, writes, sources and corrects its articles.">
  <div class="page">
<h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
<p>Health information affects real decisions, so we hold ourselves to clear standards.</p>
<h2>1. Sources first</h2>
<p>Every article is based on reputable sources: government health agencies (NIH, CDC, NHS, WHO), peer-reviewed research, and established academic medical centers. Each article cites at least three sources. We avoid citing blogs, product sellers or anonymous forums.</p>
<h2>2. What we cover</h2>
<p>Educational, evergreen topics: how organs and systems work, sleep science, nutrition fundamentals, the benefits of movement, the brain, and common "why does this happen?" questions.</p>
<h2>3. What we do not cover</h2>
<ul>
<li>Medication or supplement dosages, or advice to start, stop or change any treatment.</li>
<li>Diagnosis of symptoms or conditions.</li>
<li>Unproven "cures", detoxes or miracle claims.</li>
<li>Sponsored content disguised as editorial.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. AI assistance and transparency</h2>
<p>We use AI tools to help research and draft articles. AI output is never published without being grounded in cited sources and checked against our quality standards for accuracy, depth and readability. We disclose this openly because we believe readers deserve to know how content is made.</p>
<h2>5. Accuracy and updates</h2>
<p>Each article shows its publication date and, when revised, its update date. We periodically review older articles to keep them aligned with current guidance.</p>
<h2>6. Corrections</h2>
<p>If you find an error, please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. We review every report and correct confirmed errors promptly, updating the article's date.</p>
<h2>7. Advertising</h2>
<p>This site may display advertising to support its operation. Advertisers have no influence over our editorial content.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
